@meta
id: 11-03
title: Drill — dw then dot, eight times
chapter: 11
type: drill
skills: dot-repeat
par: 23
minutes: 5
reps: 8
vscode: eight double-clicks and eight Deletes
open: review.md

@brief
Eight review comments, each one still labelled `draft`. Strip the label
off all eight. One `dw`, then `j.` to the bottom of the file.

Nothing to work out. The rhythm is the whole exercise: move, dot, move,
dot, without looking down at your hands.

@keys
| `dw` | delete the word and the space after it |
| `j` | down a line, same column |
| `.` | again |

@hints
`dw` once at the top. After that you are only ever pressing two keys per
line.
--
Do not re-type `dw` on line two. If your fingers want to, that is exactly
the habit this drill exists to break.

@solution
    dw  then  j.  seven times

Twenty keystrokes for eight edits, and only one of them was a decision.

The reason to drill this rather than just know it: `.` pays off when it is
automatic. The moment you have to stop and ask "is the dot still holding
what I want?" you have already lost the time it saves. After a few hundred
repetitions your hand reaches for `j.` before the thought finishes, and
that is when repeated edits stop being work.

@start review.md
draft the refund guard needs a test for the boundary day
draft this catch swallows the payment error, log it
draft customerId is read twice, hoist it
draft prefer the repository over a raw prisma call here
draft this string should come from the errors file
draft the retry count is duplicated in three places
draft toEntity should not expose the deleted column
draft name this after the class, not the interface

@expected review.md
the refund guard needs a test for the boundary day
this catch swallows the payment error, log it
customerId is read twice, hoist it
prefer the repository over a raw prisma call here
this string should come from the errors file
the retry count is duplicated in three places
toEntity should not expose the deleted column
name this after the class, not the interface
